ce  of the kid okay not the old man looking  back i love it but but when dustin  hoffman and robert redford uh came to  play woodward and me  they  they lived with us for like six months i  mean not they didn't come back to our  houses at night but every day they spent  with us practically uh dustin he came he  came to the passover seder  at home we would walk around the city  together  we really got to know each other well  and  and  redford and hoffman would ask us  question after question after question  how did you do this how what would what  were your exact words be to each other  and and the great director of the movie  alan kakula followed uh what we had said  the only reason we were willing to sell  the rights to  the book of all the president's members  is if indeed  a movie director and a producer was  willing to stick to the absolute truth  of what that book was and and not  fictionalize it and uh and that's indeed  what what allen pakula the great  director did that's what redford did  that's what hoffman did and one of the  extraordinary things about all the  presidents  and again there's a straight line from  this chasing history book to  uh  to all the president's men in the  watergate coverages  that  the movie and the book the movie  especially of all the president's men  it's not about woodward and me in our  personal lives there's no love story in  it we don't see any girlfriends it's  about the process of reporting  and what a news organization does  and it's again i mean one of these today  in our journalism there's an awful lot  too much  going to google not getting out of the  newsroom and thinking oh i can go to  google and learn all about what's online  and learn people's biographies and  everything about it if google and  wikipedia are right and they're not  always right  but google and the internet are fabulous  reporting tools but they're no  substitute for what you see in that  movie of all the president's men or what  you read in chasing history it's all  about going out and meeting people and  respecting the people you're covering  even if they're if you even if they're  lying to you you know usually people  tell you what they they're  their truth it might not be the truth  but it's your responsibility to then go  to other sources and find out is what  they think is their truth really the  truth and their shades of gray all  through this that's what all the  presidents and then the movie is about  that's what chasing history the book is  so it's really almost  a prequel to all the president's men  because you see this 16 year old kid  that's 17 year old 18 19 learning and  learning and getting these opportunities  uh  that you can could not even imagine  today  you know one of the first weeks that i  was at the paper  it's right before  john kennedy was elected running against  richard nixon  i was asked by  by the head copy was asked i was told go  to burning tree country club we've got a  photographer out there taking pictures  of president eisenhower on the golf  course and i want you to bring back to  the office his rolls of film  so i went out to burning street country  club  and i had a little identification card  from the washington star and i showed it  to the chief caddy and he took me right  to this putting green and 12 feet away  from me with just one secret service man  nearby was the president of the united  states dwight eisenhower sinking butts  and  and you know i was mesmerized i looked  at his hands and and i could see that  you know they had brown spots on his  hands and i made a little dump in my  notebook and the photographer took the  film out of his camera and  gave it to me and and i left it was the  first time i'd ever seen a president of  the united states but imagine that at  age 16 and a few weeks later  a week before the election jack kennedy  came to my high school in silver spring  maryland and the state editor said to me  well ernst you know we've got our top  political reporter covering this event  because the election is going to be very  close between the candidate and nixon  and maryland could even decide the  election so our top political reporter  is going to come to your high school but  why don't you go stay on the grounds and  again cover the crowd and you know the  ground is there and you know the  community and 12 000 people showed up  for this rally and so even in the first  few weeks of my apprenticeship  at the washington star and you can  imagine some of my classmates looking  bug-eyed at me as i'm sitting there with  a reporter's notebook right running  behind behind the candidate for  president of the united states i mean  what you know what a  good thing to be able to do looking back  at it it's this amazing life that you  know that i've been lucky enough to have  but still this occurring at that age is  you know i'm kind of against myself at  the opportunity and what happened in  those years from age 16 to 21. well  congrats on the book uh chasing history  kid in the newsroom greatly appreciate  this chat love to have you on again carl  bernstein and thank you for all your  work  thank you very much  appreciate it that's carl bernstein  everybody check out his book hey you  watched all the way to the end thanks  for that watch more right here
 

